// T4 map release, the NEGATIVE case: the destructor must NEVER free a key.
//
// goo_map_set_sv stores the caller's key pointer VERBATIM (`e->key = k;`) and
// never duplicates it, so the map does not own key storage. A key can be a
// string literal's constant data in .rodata, and freeing one would hand a
// constant to free(). goo_map_delete_sv records this rule and goo_map_clear_sv
// applies it to the whole entry chain; goo_map_dtor is the GooObjDtor adapter
// over that same walk. This probe is what pins the rule at the release site.
//
// `key` IS A CONCATENATION AND NOT A LITERAL, and that is load-bearing. A
// literal carries GOO_RC_IMMORTAL and goo_release returns early on one, so a
// wrongly freed literal key would be a silent no-op and this probe would prove
// nothing. A concat buffer is ordinary goo_alloc memory with a real header, so a
// wrong free lands on the heap where valgrind can see it. The same trap is
// recorded on examples/arc_release_substring_probe.goo.
//
// `use` DESTROYS ITS MAP A THOUSAND TIMES while main holds the one key they all
// share. THEN main reads it. If goo_map_dtor ever freed keys, the first
// destruction frees `key`, the remaining 999 map writes touch freed memory, and
// the final println reads it — an invalid read and an invalid free, both of
// which the arc-release-probe gate greps for.
//
// A single shared key across every iteration is deliberate. A fresh key per
// iteration would still leak-check clean if keys were freed, because each key
// would die with its own map.
//
// IMPORT-FREE, for the reason the other arc probes record.
package main

func use(k string) int {
	counts := map[string]int{}
	counts[k] = 1
	return len(counts)
}

func main() {
	key := "abc" + "def"
	total := 0
	for i := 0; i < 1000; i++ {
		total = total + use(key)
	}
	println(key)
	println(total)
}
